//! lotus/halve_neg.rye — ALES86: the inverted half-wave rectifier — keep the negative half, zero the positive, y = min(x, 0). The half-wave rectifier
//! (ALES85) keeps the POSITIVE half of the wave and silences the negative (y = max(x, 0)); this rung is its mirror — it keeps the NEGATIVE half and
//! silences the positive. A half-wave rectifier built from a single diode oriented one way passes the positive half; flip the diode and it passes the
//! negative half. Together the two half-wave rectifiers PARTITION the wave: a sample lands in exactly one of them (its zero shared), and adding their two
//! outputs reconstructs the original wave sample for sample — max(x, 0) + min(x, 0) = x, the plainest decomposition of a signal into its positive and
//! negative parts.
//!
//! The crux mirrors ALES85's exactly: where max(x, 0) is exact for every i16 with no rail edge (a negative sample becomes 0, a non-negative passes),
//! min(x, 0) is likewise EXACT for EVERY i16 with NO rail edge — a non-positive sample passes unchanged (already within [sample_min, 0]) and a positive
//! sample becomes 0; neither branch can overflow, and sample_min passes THROUGH untouched (it is already a legal i16, unlike the full-wave rectifier
//! where its magnitude 32768 overflowed the positive rail). The inverted half-wave rectifier needs NO saturate.
//!
//! Harmonically it is the same even+odd mix as ALES85, sign-flipped: min(x, 0) = (x − |x|) / 2 carries the fundamental (odd) plus the even harmonics
//! minus a DC term (the offset points the other way — a one-sided signal below zero rather than above). The map is MEMORYLESS — every output depends
//! only on its own input, so the in-place read-then-write touches nothing another step needs, and halving a span block by block equals halving it whole.
//! It is IDEMPOTENT — a non-positive sample maps to itself, so halve_neg(halve_neg(x)) = halve_neg(x). And its output is always NON-POSITIVE — the
//! below-zero one-sided signal is the inverted rectifier's whole point.
//!
//! Season: the Six-Season double-seat, Season C thread (Lotus · the creative suite) · waymark ALES · rung ALES86.
//! Design read: active-designing/date/20260814/20260814-214941_fill-ales86-lotus-inverted-half-wave-rectifier.md
//! Stands on: lotus/timeline.rye (ALES2) — the Clip, sample_min/max. It carries NO gain, NO ceiling, and NO threshold — the threshold is fixed at zero,
//! and min(x, 0) has no parameter that could be illegal; a keeper who wants the two halves separately runs ALES85's halve and this halve_neg over the
//! same span, both in-place maps whose outputs sum back to the dry wave.
//!
//! HONEST SCOPE — software only, purely LOCAL. Bounded in-process i16 PCM buffers on one bench, siloed to lotus/. No socket, no network, no keys, no
//! funds, no real device, no real sample rate — the shape is an inverted half-wave threshold at zero, instantaneous (no attack/release, no
//! anti-aliasing). One compare-and-keep per sample; no saturate is needed, since min(x, 0) never leaves the rail.
//!
//!   env RYE_ZIG=vendor/zig-toolchain/zig rye/bin/rye run lotus/halve_neg.rye selftest

const std = @import("std");
const assert = std.debug.assert;
const print = std.debug.print;

const timeline = @import("timeline.rye");

pub const halve_neg_version = "20260814.214941";

/// The inverted half-wave rectifier's faults, one consistent name each. BadRange coincides by name with the positive half-wave rectifier, the full-wave
/// rectifier, the sibling drives, and ALES2's timeline (Zig merges error sets by name), so a composer sees one consistent span fault. It names NO other
/// fault — it takes no gain, no ceiling, no threshold: min(x, 0) has no parameter that could be illegal.
pub const HalveNegError = error{
    /// A span outside the current samples — refused, no write.
    BadRange,
};

/// Inverted-half-wave-rectify `count` samples starting at `start` in place in `clip`: each sample becomes `min(x, 0)`, the negative half of the wave
/// kept whole and the positive half folded away to silence. Exact for EVERY i16 — a non-positive sample passes unchanged, a positive sample becomes 0;
/// there is no rail edge and no saturate (sample_min passes through, unlike the full-wave rectifier where its magnitude overflowed the positive rail).
/// After the call every output is non-positive, at least the input, and depends only on its own input (the rectifier is memoryless). Refuses BadRange on
/// a span outside the samples — BEFORE any write, so an illegal call leaves the clip exactly as it was. Values only — never the length.
pub fn halve_neg(clip: *timeline.Clip, start: u32, count: u32) HalveNegError!void {
    // invariant: the span lies wholly within the current samples — checked without underflow (count vs the remainder), exactly as the positive sibling.
    if (start > clip.len) return error.BadRange;
    if (count > clip.len - start) return error.BadRange;

    var k: u32 = 0;
    while (k < count) : (k += 1) {
        const i: u32 = start + k;
        const dry: i16 = clip.buf[i];
        // invariant: the sample read is a legal i16 — it is audio, never a wide intermediate.
        assert(dry >= timeline.sample_min and dry <= timeline.sample_max);
        // The inverted half-wave map: keep the negative half, silence the positive. No wide intermediate and no saturate are needed — a non-positive
        // sample is already within [sample_min, 0], and a positive sample becomes 0, so min(x, 0) never leaves [sample_min, 0]. sample_min passes
        // through untouched, the mirror of ALES85 where sample_max passed through.
        const out: i16 = if (dry < 0) dry else 0;
        // invariant: the output is non-positive — an inverted rectifier makes a below-zero one-sided signal, so the positive half is silenced.
        assert(out <= 0);
        // invariant: the output is exactly the inverted half-wave map — a negative sample passes unchanged, a non-negative one rises to 0 — so the map
        // only holds or raises, never lowers, and touches only the sign of the wave.
        assert(if (dry < 0) out == dry else out == 0);
        clip.buf[i] = out;
    }
    // invariant: an inverted halve changes sample values only, never the clip's length or bound.
    assert(start + count <= clip.len);
    assert(clip.len <= clip.buf.len);
}

// --- selftest: NON-POSITIVES ARE UNCHANGED (min(x,0) = x for x ≤ 0); POSITIVES FOLD to SILENCE by hand (+x → 0); NO RAIL EDGE (sample_min PASSES
//     through, never a saturate — the mirror of ALES85's sample_max); every output is NON-POSITIVE and the map is IDEMPOTENT; the two half-waves
//     PARTITION the wave (halve + halve_neg = the dry wave, sample for sample); the SPAN discipline holds; and a span past the end refuses BadRange, the
//     clip untouched ---

fn eq(got: []const i16, want: []const i16) bool {
    if (got.len != want.len) return false;
    var i: u32 = 0;
    while (i < want.len) : (i += 1) if (got[i] != want[i]) return false;
    return true;
}

fn load_clip(clip: *timeline.Clip, src: []const i16) !void {
    try timeline.splice(clip, 0, src);
}

fn run_selftest() !u8 {
    // 1. NON-POSITIVES ARE UNCHANGED — min(x, 0) = x for every x ≤ 0, so the negative half of the wave passes through whole. A signal already one-sided
    //    below zero inverted-half-wave-rectifies to itself.
    {
        const sig = [_]i16{ 0, -1, -12345, timeline.sample_min, -200, -5 };
        var clip = timeline.Clip{};
        try load_clip(&clip, &sig);
        try halve_neg(&clip, 0, clip.len);
        if (!eq(clip.samples(), &sig)) return error.NonPosChanged;
    }

    // 2. POSITIVES FOLD TO SILENCE BY HAND — each positive sample drops to 0, the positive half of the wave folded away. 1→0, 100→0, 12345→0, 32767→0;
    //    the negatives among them pass unchanged.
    {
        const sig = [_]i16{ 1, -100, 100, 12345, -32767, 32767 };
        var clip = timeline.Clip{};
        try load_clip(&clip, &sig);
        try halve_neg(&clip, 0, clip.len);
        const want = [_]i16{ 0, -100, 0, 0, -32767, 0 };
        if (!eq(clip.samples(), &want)) return error.FoldWrong;
    }

    // 3. NO RAIL EDGE — where the full-wave rectifier had to SATURATE sample_min, the inverted half-wave rectifier passes sample_min straight THROUGH:
    //    min(sample_min, 0) = sample_min, since it is already non-positive and a legal i16. sample_max folds to 0. min(x, 0) is EXACT for every i16 — the
    //    mirror of ALES85, where sample_max passed and sample_min silenced.
    {
        const sig = [_]i16{ timeline.sample_min, -32767, 0, 32767 };
        var clip = timeline.Clip{};
        try load_clip(&clip, &sig);
        try halve_neg(&clip, 0, clip.len);
        const want = [_]i16{ timeline.sample_min, -32767, 0, 0 }; // sample_min passes (exact); 32767 → 0 (silenced)
        if (!eq(clip.samples(), &want)) return error.RailEdgeWrong;
    }

    // 4. EVERY OUTPUT IS NON-POSITIVE, AND THE MAP IS IDEMPOTENT — an inverted rectifier makes a below-zero one-sided signal, so no output is above zero;
    //    and inverted-rectifying the already-rectified clip a second time changes nothing (a non-positive sample maps to itself).
    {
        const sig = [_]i16{ 100, -200, 5000, -5000, timeline.sample_min, 0, 1, 32767 };
        var clip = timeline.Clip{};
        try load_clip(&clip, &sig);
        try halve_neg(&clip, 0, clip.len);
        const once = clip.samples();
        var i: u32 = 0;
        while (i < once.len) : (i += 1) if (once[i] > 0) return error.PositiveOutput;
        var again = timeline.Clip{};
        try load_clip(&again, once);
        try halve_neg(&again, 0, again.len);
        if (!eq(again.samples(), once)) return error.NotIdempotent;
    }

    // 5. THE TWO HALF-WAVES PARTITION THE WAVE — for every sample, ALES85's halve (positive half) plus this halve_neg (negative half) reconstructs the
    //    dry wave exactly: max(x, 0) + min(x, 0) = x. Compute the positive half by hand, the negative half by this map, and prove their sum is the
    //    original sample for sample — the plainest decomposition of a signal into its positive and negative parts.
    {
        const sig = [_]i16{ 6000, -6000, 300, -300, 0, timeline.sample_min, 32767, -1 };
        // the positive half by hand (ALES85's max(x, 0)), the negative half by this map (min(x, 0))
        const pos = [_]i16{ 6000, 0, 300, 0, 0, 0, 32767, 0 };
        var clip = timeline.Clip{};
        try load_clip(&clip, &sig);
        try halve_neg(&clip, 0, clip.len);
        const neg = clip.samples();
        var i: u32 = 0;
        while (i < sig.len) : (i += 1) {
            const sum: i32 = @as(i32, pos[i]) + @as(i32, neg[i]);
            if (sum != @as(i32, sig[i])) return error.NotPartitioned; // the two halves sum to the dry wave, sample for sample
        }
    }

    // 6. THE SPAN DISCIPLINE — inverted-rectifying a sub-span leaves samples outside [start, count) exactly as they were, the in-place discipline the
    //    whole suite keeps. Halve the middle two of six; the first and last two are untouched, positives among them preserved.
    {
        const sig = [_]i16{ 100, 200, -500, 500, 300, 400 };
        var clip = timeline.Clip{};
        try load_clip(&clip, &sig);
        try halve_neg(&clip, 2, 2); // indices 2,3 only: −500→−500, 500→0
        const want = [_]i16{ 100, 200, -500, 0, 300, 400 };
        if (!eq(clip.samples(), &want)) return error.SpanLeaked;
    }

    // 7. A SPAN past the end refuses BadRange — before any write, the clip untouched. The inverted half-wave rectifier names no other fault (min(x, 0)
    //    has no parameter that could be illegal).
    {
        const sig = [_]i16{ 9, -10, 11 };
        var clip = timeline.Clip{};
        try load_clip(&clip, &sig);
        if (halve_neg(&clip, 1, 3)) |_| return error.OverRangeSlipped else |e| if (e != error.BadRange) return error.WrongOverRange;
        if (!eq(clip.samples(), &sig)) return error.RefusedOverRangeTouched;
    }

    print("GREEN ales-halve-neg: the INVERTED HALF-WAVE RECTIFIER — keep the negative half, zero the positive, y = min(x, 0). The half-wave rectifier " ++
        "(ALES85) keeps the POSITIVE half and silences the negative (y = max(x, 0)); this rung is its mirror — it keeps the NEGATIVE half and silences " ++
        "the positive. A single diode oriented one way passes the positive half; flip it and it passes the negative. Together the two half-wave " ++
        "rectifiers PARTITION the wave: max(x, 0) + min(x, 0) = x, the plainest decomposition of a signal into its positive and negative parts. The " ++
        "crux mirrors ALES85's: min(x, 0) is EXACT for EVERY i16 with NO rail edge — a non-positive sample passes unchanged, a positive sample becomes " ++
        "0, neither branch overflows, and NO saturate is needed; sample_min passes THROUGH untouched (unlike the full-wave rectifier, where its " ++
        "magnitude overflowed the positive rail). Harmonically it is the even+odd mix sign-flipped: min(x, 0) = (x − |x|) / 2 carries the fundamental " ++
        "plus the even harmonics minus a DC term (the offset points below zero). Non-positives pass unchanged; positives fold to silence (1→0, 100→0, " ++
        "12345→0, 32767→0); there is no rail edge (sample_min passes, sample_max silences); every output is NON-POSITIVE and the map is IDEMPOTENT (a " ++
        "non-positive sample maps to itself); the two half-waves partition the wave (halve + halve_neg = the dry wave, sample for sample); the span " ++
        "discipline holds; a span past the end refuses BadRange, the clip untouched. The map is MEMORYLESS; one compare-and-keep per sample; no gain, " ++
        "no ceiling, no threshold, no saturate, no delay line, no anti-aliasing, no socket, no network, no keys, no funds, no real device, no real " ++
        "sample rate. The RECTIFIER FAMILY now stands whole — full-wave (pure even), positive half-wave and inverted half-wave (the even+odd mix, the " ++
        "two halves that partition the wave) — beside the DRIVE clippers\n", .{});
    return 0;
}

pub fn main(init: std.process.Init) !u8 {
    _ = init;
    return run_selftest();
}
